From 5258d852f5962e47335c1fb17cc9e3253852aa78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 16:08:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02616/11362] [outer-loop] config rule-inducer step 1:
 interaction vocabulary is LEARNABLE (2-3 types) + explains Stage 2 fail

First concrete step of the operator's config rule-INDUCER direction: introspect the config games'
INTERACTION model (the meta-learned library's foundation). Measured per game whether clicking a cell
cycles it and whether keyboard changes the grid.

KEY FINDING: clicking a cell NEVER changes that cell's colour in ANY of the 9 config games -- the
interaction is INDIRECT (a control click toggles a target elsewhere, or keyboard cycles a SELECTED
element). This is exactly WHY the Stage 2 executor failed: it assumed DIRECT cell-recolor (click cell
-> set its colour), the wrong model for every config game.

The config games share a SMALL LEARNABLE interaction vocabulary, NOT bespoke per-game mechanics:
  - keyboard-cycle/move-selected: tr87, ka59, dc22, lf52, bp35
  - click-toggle-indirect: sc25, tn36, s5i5
  - keyboard-needs-selection-first: g50t

POSITIVE for the rule-inducer: a meta-learned library of interaction patterns IS viable (introspection
can classify a new game's interaction type from ~2-3 shared types). This is the first piece. Next:
model each interaction type, induce the win-RULE on top, and rebuild the executor with the CORRECT
(indirect/selected) interaction model. Validates the operator's direction over per-game adapters.
results/arc3_config_interaction_vocabulary.json. Adversarial-verify: clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
